  "reasoning": "The statement is incorrect. The provided regulations do not prohibit physical examination of AEO consignments under all circumstances. They grant AEO holders reduced selection rates and priority (e.g., Clause 11(1)–(2) reductions/selectivity and Green Lane automatic clearance percentages: minimum 50%/75%/90% reductions in selection rates and Green Lane minimums 20%/30%/50% with an additional 10% when using designated logistics operators — see DOCUMENTS 1, 2, 3). However, the rules explicitly allow physical examination or inspection in specific circumstances: trustworthy intelligence or suspicion permits physical examination (Clause 11(3) in DOCUMENTS 1, 2, 3), discovery of irregularity on an AEO holder’s consignment triggers immediate selection and suspension of privileges (Clause 11(3)), and customs may select consignments for on-site physical examination with written reasons (DOCUMENT 3 Clause 11(3)). Annual onsite post-clearance audits and ad hoc audits/investigations on suspicion are also authorized (Clause 14 in DOCUMENTS 7, 8, 9). DOCUMENT 6 lists AEO privileges (including possibility of physical exam at AEO premises), which further demonstrates examination remains possible. Therefore AEO status reduces and streamlines examinations but does not create an absolute prohibition on physical examination under any circumstances.",
  "verdict": "INCORRECT"
}

